onal curvature, and more generally, certain quotients of universal spaces for families. The result is\nput into context with the Nielsen realisation problem for aspherical manifolds, and we give a unifying\naccount of different formulations of that problem, made possible by the same methods.\nContents\n1.\nIntroduction\n1\n2.\nQuotients of universal spaces for families\n4\n3.\nActions in nonpositive curvature\n6\nReferences\n7\n1. Introduction\nLet M be a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature. Assume the finite\ngroup G acts on M via isometries. For each subgroup H ⊂G, the fixed submanifold M H ⊂M is\nan embedded submanifold, which in fact inherits a Riemannian metric with nonpositive sectional\ncurvature as well. This submanifold will be referred to as the genuine H-fixed points of the action.\nThis article is about the following homotopical property of such actions, comparing the genuine fixed\npoints of M to the homotopy fixed points.\nTheorem 1.1. If M is a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature and an\nisometric action by a finite group G, then for each H ≤G the canonical map\nM H →M hH\nfrom the genuine H-fixed points of M to the homotopy H-fixed points, is an equivalence.\narXiv:2510.21550v1  [math.GT]  24 Oct 2025\nRecall that a G-space X is a Borel G-space if for each H ≤G the map XH →XhH is an equiv-\nalence. So §1.1 predicts that nonpositively curved manifolds with isometric G-action are Borel G-\nspaces. In this note, we record a proof of §1.1 in terms of genuine equivariant homotopy theory, de-\npending on the Cartan-Hadamard theorem from differential geometry as geometric input. The proof\nis achieved by translating - using said theorem from geometry - to a result about certain quotients of\nuniversal spaces for families. That result is §1.3, and should in fact be viewed as the main mathemat-\nical content of this note. We briefly explain §1.3 and put it into context with the Nielsen realisation\nproblem after that.\nDate: October 27, 2025.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Borel actions in nonpositively curved geometry and the Nielsen realisation problem (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/396923957_Borel_actions_in_nonpositively_curved_geometry_and_the_Nielsen_realisation_problem)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Borel actions in nonpositively curved geometry and the Nielsen realisation problem ... In this note, we record the proof of a theorem about the coincidence of genuine and homotopy fixed points for isometric group actions on complete Riemannian manifolds with nonpositive sectional curvature, and more generally, certain quotients of universal spaces for families. ... So §1.1 predicts that nonpositively curved manifolds with isometric G-action are Borel G- ... In: (2024). arXiv: 2409.\n\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Borel actions in nonpositively curved geometry and the Nielsen realisation problem\n\n  * October 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2510.21550\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nChristian Kremer\n\nChristian Kremer\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this note, we record the proof of a theorem about the coincidence of genuine and homotopy fixed points for isometric group actions on complete Riemannian manifolds with nonpositive sectional curvature, and more generally, certain quotients of universal spaces for families. The result is put into context with the Nielsen realisation problem for aspherical manifolds, and we give a unifying account of different formulations of that problem, made possible by the same methods.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nBOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSEN\n\nREALISATION PROBLEM\n\nCHRISTIAN KREMER\n\nAbstract. In this note, we record the proof of a theorem about the coincidence of genuine and ho-\n\nmotopy xed points for isometric group actions on complete Riemannian manifolds with nonpositive\n\nsectional curvature, and more generally, certain quotients of universal spaces for families. e result is\n\nput into context with the Nielsen realisation problem for aspherical manifolds, and we give a unifying\n\naccount of dierent formulations of that problem, made possible by the same methods.\n\nContents\n\n1. Introduction 1\n\n2. otients of universal spaces for families 4\n\n3. Actions in nonpositive curvature 6\n\nReferences 7\n\n1. Introduction\n\nLet M be a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature. Assume the nite\n\ngroup G acts on M via isometries. For each subgroup H⊂G, the xed submanifold M H⊂M is\n\nan embedded submanifold, which in fact inherits a Riemannian metric with nonpositive sectional\n\ncurvature as well. is submanifold will be referred to as the genuine H-xed points of the action.\n\nis article is about the following homotopical property of such actions, comparing the genuine xed\n\npoints of M to the homotopy xed points.\n\neorem 1.1. If M is a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature and an\n\nisometric action by a nite group G, then for each H≤G the canonical map\n\nM H→M hH\n\nfrom the genuine H-xed points of M to the homotopy H-xed points, is an equivalence.\n\nRecall that a G-space X isa Borel G-space if for each H≤G the map X H→X hH is an equiv-\n\nalence. So §1.1 predicts that nonpositively curved manifolds with isometric G-action are Borel G-\n\nspaces. In this note, we record a proof of §1.1 in terms of genuine equivariant homotopy theory, de-\n\npending on the Cartan-Hadamard theorem from dierential geometry as geometric input. e proof\n\nis achieved by translating - using said theorem from geometry - to a result about certain quotients of\n\nuniversal spaces for families. at result is §1.3, and should in fact be viewed as the main mathemat-\n\nical content of this note. We briey explain §1.3 and put it into context with the Nielsen realisation\n\nproblem aer that.\n\nDate: October 27, 2025.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2510.21550v1 [math.GT] 24 Oct 2025\n\nBOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSEN REALISATION PROBLEM 2\n\nRecall that for a discrete group Γ, a family of subgroups is a collection F of subgroups closed under\n\nconjugation and passing to subgroups. Given such a family F, a universal Γ-space for the family F is\n\nthere is an equivalence of categories\n\nAn F\n\nΓ≃(An G)/Bor(Bπ)\n\nwhich sends X∈An F\n\nΓ to π\\X, where the map π\\X→Bor(Bπ)comes from the action of π on X\n\nbeing free, so that we have the G-equivariant map (π\\X)e≃X hπ → ∗hπ ≃Bπ.\n\nBOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSEN REALISATION PROBLEM 6\n\nProof. Recall that if C is a small category, and X∈ P(C)is a presheaf, then P(C)/X ≃ P(C/X ),\n\nwhere C/X =C ×P(C)P(C)/X . Applying this to (An G)/Bor(Bπ )we get an equivalence\n\n(An G)/Bor(Bπ)≃ P (O(G)×An G(An G)/Bor(Bπ)).\n\nSince An F\n\nΓ≃ P(O F(Γ)), we have reduced to provide an equivalence O F(Γ) ≃ O(G)×An G\n\n(An G)/Bor(Bπ). e claim follows from the following commuting diagram of cartesian squares.\n\n(6)\n\nO F(Γ) Asph inj,F\n\n/B Γ An/B Γ≃(An B G)/Bπ (An G)/Bor(Bπ)\n\nO(G) Asph inj\n\n/BG An/BG An G\n\nπ\\−\n\nBor\n\nBor\n\ne rst square is consists of equivalences in the horizontal direction as in §2.4. e second square is\n\ncartesian as a consequence of §2.5, and the last square by §2.6.□\n\nCorollary 2.9. In the situation of §2.8 isa G-equivariant equivalence\n\nπ\\E F Γ≃Bor(Bπ).\n\nProof. e equivalence of categories in §2.8 has to send the terminal object of An F\n\nΓ, that is E F Γ,\n\nto the terminal object of (An G)/Bor(Bπ), which is clearly Bor(Bπ). But the explicit description of\n\nthat equivalence shows that it is implemented by taking the quotient by the π-action, leading to the\n\nassertion. □\n\n3. Actions in nonpositive curvature\n\nIn the following we will abbreviate\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSEN (https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_3676542_1/component/file_3676543/2510.21550.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; BOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSENREALISATION PROBLEMCHRISTIAN KREMERarXiv:2512.01501v1 [math.CT] 24 Oct 2025ABSTRACT. ... If \\(M\\) is a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature and an isometric action by a finite group \\(G\\), then for each \\(H \\le G\\) the canonical map\\(M^H \\to M^{hH}\\)from the genuine $H$-fixed points of $M$ to the homotopy $H$-fixed points, is an equivalence.Recall that a G-space X is a Borel G-space if for each H ≤ G the map X^H → X^{hH} is an equivalence.\nBOREL ACTIONS IN NONPOSITIVELY CURVED GEOMETRY AND THE NIELSEN\nREALISATION PROBLEMCHRISTIAN KREMERarXiv:2512.01501v1 [math.CT] 24 Oct 2025ABSTRACT. In this note, we record the proof of a theorem about the coincidence of genuine and homotopy fixed points for isometric group actions on complete Riemannian manifolds with nonpositive sectional curvature, and more generally, certain quotients of universal spaces for families. The result is put into context with the Nielsen realisation problem for aspherical manifolds, and we give a unifying account of different formulations of that problem, made possible by the same methods.CONTENTS1.  Introduction 1\n2.  Quotients of universal spaces for families 4\n3.  Actions in nonpositive curvature 6\nReferences 71. INTRODUCTIONLet \\(M\\) be a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature. Assume the finite\ngroup \\(G\\) acts on \\(M\\) via isometries. For each subgroup \\(H \\subset G\\), the fixed submanifold \\(M^{H} \\subset M\\) is\nan embedded submanifold, which in fact inherits a Riemannian metric with nonpositive sectional\ncurvature as well. This submanifold will be referred to as the genuine \\(H\\)-fixed points of the action.\nThis article is about the following homotopical property of such actions, comparing the genuine fixed\npoints of \\(M\\) to the homotopy fixed points.Theorem 1.1.  If \\(M\\) is a complete Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature and an isometric action by a finite group \\(G\\), then for each \\(H \\le G\\) the canonical map\\(M^H \\to M^{hH}\\)from the genuine $H$-fixed points of $M$ to the homotopy $H$-fixed points, is an equivalence.Recall that a G-space X is a Borel G-space if for each H ≤ G the map X^H → X^{hH} is an equivalence. So §1.1 predicts that nonpositively curved manifolds with isometric G-action are Borel G-\nspaces. In this note, we record a proof of §1.1 in terms of genuine equivariant homotopy theory, de-\npending on the Cartan-Hadamard theorem from differential geometry as geometric input. The proof\nis achieved by translating - using said theorem from geometry - to a result about certain quotients of\nuniversal spaces for families. That result is §1.3, and should in fact be viewed as the main mathemat-\nical content of this note. We briefly explain §1.3 and put it into context with the Nielsen realisation\nproblem after that.Date: October 27, 2025.1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Kremer_C in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKremer_C+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Borel actions in nonpositively curved geometry and the Nielsen realisation problem ... @misc{2510.21550, author = {Christian Kremer}, titl